Description: Enigma2 IPTV player with provider TV archive and XMLTV EPG
 Native Enigma2 interface for Xtream Codes, local or HTTP(S) M3U playlists,
 and Stalker/MAC portals. M3U sources support optional background XMLTV EPG;
 Xtream XMLTV data can also be mapped safely to native DVB services.
 Selectable TXT banks support up to 100 arbitrary file paths each for Xtream
 and Stalker/MAC, with remote-controlled browsing and immediate activation.
 The source manager automatically checks Xtream and Stalker/MAC accounts,
 reports expiry and connection usage with coloured status lines, and offers
 confirmed inactive accounts for explicit bulk-deletion approval.
 The yellow key opens the existing traffic-light health screen for only the
 selected Xtream, Stalker/MAC or M3U source. Automatic account testing and its
 EXIT cancellation remain available; OK connects and M3U blue opens XMLTV EPG.
 Traffic-light artwork retains transparent edges without black rectangles.
 Stalker Live TV requests native short EPG with the real channel ID; empty
 channels do not disable later EPG discovery. Bounded fallback queries and
 per-channel caching preserve responsive preview and fullscreen playback.
 Current programmes retain valid duration/next-start end times; portal date
 pairs and decimal Unix timestamps are normalized before NOW/NEXT selection.
 Future-only responses permit one bounded alternative query for current EPG.
 Stalker EPG starts after a shorter playback settling interval. Fullscreen
 CH+/CH- refreshes native Stalker and Xtream EPG in the background and updates
 the infobar; cancelled or late replies cannot replace another channel's EPG.
 TV archive first-open testing releases the previous service and waits 500 ms
 before resolving its archive link. The selected playback engine is retained;
 cancellation or link failure restores the previous service when still owned.
 M3U blue-key EPG settings guidance is localized in all 43 UI catalogs.
 The welcome card also checks the selected account expiry and connection usage
 in the background with a strict three-second budget.
 The fullscreen OK key toggles the information bar without changing playback.
 Movies and series optionally use the same cinematic layout with twelve
 category rows, one continuous full-bleed hero, a five-card rail and slowly
 scrolling long summaries. Detail text now uses change-only native updates,
 avoiding duplicate transparent-layer repaints while provider information is
 loading. SF8008-family receivers render one-pixel/200 ms synopsis motion on a
 fixed software canvas instead of moving a native child widget; other receivers
 retain the approved one-pixel/80 ms native motion. Bounded text gradients
 preserve faces without dimming the complete artwork.
 Stalker/MAC artwork uses
 portal detail, explicit wide aliases and verified TMDb enrichment: generic
 catalogue numbers are ignored, while explicit IDs must match the selected
 title/year before their hero can be displayed. Portrait posters remain cards.
 If no verified wide hero exists, the transparent hero surface reveals the
 normal application background under bounded alpha text shadows; posters are
 never enlarged. Movie and Series category paint reaches the physical left
 edge and meets the hero without a visible seam.
 Explicit background layers prevent the active DVB service from showing
 through; the appearance setting restores the classic VOD layout.
 TV Archive replays available provider recordings through Xtream Codes,
 native Stalker/MAC archive APIs, or declared M3U catchup with XMLTV history.
 The neon interface follows the receiver language through 43 catalogs.
